  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 222 AM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nSatellite shows a weak shortwave pushing across Colorado in these\nearly morning hours, with scattered showers and a couple\nthunderstorms along and ahead of it. While these will attempt to \nmove onto the plains this morning with the associated lift, most \nof these will weaken or dissipate in the downslope westerly flow \naloft. That stronger flow develops today as a deeper upper level \nlow tracks across the Northern Rockies. Water vapor imagery  \nindicates drying behind our first shortwave, so the chances of \nprecipitation are generally decreasing after this early morning\ndisturbance. That said, we will still have some low level \nmoisture and weak instability (MLCAPE 500 J/kg or less) over the \nwestern slope and plains mainly southeast of I-76. That's where \nwe'll keep the highest PoPs (30-40%), but lower PoPs (10-20%) \nelsewhere. Temperatures will still be quite warm given downslope \nflow, and increasing westerly breezes. In fact, some gusts near 50\nmph can be expected over the Front Range mountains, with a few\ngusts (outside of showers) to around 30 mph possibly reaching\nBoulder. The stronger downslope component should boost highs to near\n90F across the plains for one last time this season (Denver has \nhad a total 65 90F degree days, the 4th most 90s since records \nbegan in 1872).\n\nCooler weather arrives tonight as a cold front pushes across the \nplains by or shortly after midnight. How much cooler for Tuesday \nis still in question. The latest data indicates low level \nmoisture and thus stratus potential or extent of any low clouds \nbehind the front would be lacking, so we'll likely have more \nsunshine and thus be able to reach the lower to mid 70s over most \nof the plains and I-25 Corridor. The lack of low level moisture \nalso means our precipitation chances, at least in the lower \nelevations, will be limited. Mountain areas should fare better \nwith at least scattered shower/storm coverage given monsoonal \nmoisture builds again in southwest flow aloft. \n\nStronger ridging occurs Wednesday as the next upper level closed \nlow digs southward along the Pacific coast. This means warmer \ntemperatures with highs likely pushing back into the mid 80s for \nthe lower elevations. Once again, the highest shower and storm \nchances will occur for the mountains given increasing moisture and\ninstability during the day, while the plains reside in a more \nmarginal environment for convection given warming aloft but some \nmoisture still available.\n\nThursday is still shaping up to be the most active day with an\nembedded shortwave and deeper monsoonal moisture lifting \nnortheast across the forecast area. Precipitable water values \nwill likely range from 150-200% of normal, so can't rule out \nlocally heavy rain. WPC Excessive Rainfall Outlook has portions of\nour forecast area in Slight Risk, and this seems reasonable, \nespecially if we are able to moisten up the low levels. \n\nThere remains plenty of uncertainty in the forecast through next\nweekend, as there is little confidence in the timing of the West\nCoast trough eventually lift east/northeast across the Northern\nand Central Rockies. As a result, this period remains rather \nbroad-brushed at this point, with continued chances of showers \nand storms and temperatures averaging near normal.",
